      "totalDuration": "Less than a year",
      "description": "Simulation Training and Rehearsal System (STARS) \n\n•\tTook the technology of VTRAT (described below) from running on two desktop computers down to running on one laptop computer. This was an Internal Research and Development (IR&D) Project.\n\n•\tDeveloped land based simulation environments using the game engine Unity 3D and integrated an updated instructional component into the system. The instructional component was integrated using a socket based network connection in Unity written in C# and allowing instruction control of the simulation in the same way as was being done in VTRAT. \n\n•\tIncorporated Virtual Reality technology through the use of an Oculus Rift head mounted display in a prototype version of VTRAT, and in the Unity 3D based simulation prototype\n",
      "insights": ""
     },
     {
      "title": "Software Engineer",
      "timePeriod": {
       "startDate": {
        "year": 1999
       },
       "endDate": {
        "year": 2014
       }
      },
      "totalDuration": "15 yrs",
      "description": "Visual Threat Recognition and Avoidance Trainer (VTRAT)\n\n•\tDeveloped software for VTRAT, a desktop trainer used at over 60 sites worldwide that teaches Air Force cargo aircraft crewmembers how to respond to threat engagements, thereby reducing potential loss of life. \n\n•\tDesigned and developed components in C++ for aircraft and threat simulation (utilizing the OpenSceneGraph / OpenGL based Delta 3D gaming and simulation engine), text-to-speech and speech recognition, and controlling all of the other components through a socket based network protocol, an instructional component written in Java.\n\n•\tWrote unit tests for the Java code in the system using the JUnit framework as part of development and system testing.\n\n•\tWrote automated instruction for the system in an XML based instructional language that included JavaScript and had a JDBC connection to MySQL and later a PostgreSQL server for storage of course structure as well as student data.\n\n•\tDeveloped and maintained scripts for startup, shutdown and backup of the system in Visual Basic script and Python to simplify use of the system by users.\n\n•\tMaintained team’s Subversion based version control system, as well as an Apache web server with an in-house PHP / MySQL based database system used for tracking the hardware and software components in use at all 60 worldwide sites, improving customer problem resolution. \n\n~ ~ ~ ADDITIONAL RELEVANT EXPERIENCE (Professional Experience prior to 1999) ~ ~ ~\n\nDeveloped and delivered the Virtual Environment Safe-for-maintenance Trainer (VEST), which was used at Sheppard Air Force Base. VEST used Virtual Reality head mounted display technology to provide a simulated environment for maintenance training of the F 15E, and it utilized an intelligent tutoring system to deliver instruction. The simulation was written in C using the Vega 3D application programming interface (API) and ran on an SGI Indigo2 workstation.\n",
      "insights": ""
     }
